    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense, the bucket idea is helpful. but honestly, part of the reason it feels so chaotic is because there are these random, tiny fragments of papers scattered everywhere—things that don't even look like full documents, just scraps or loose notes that seem to have been shoved into the corners of boxes. it feels like they're just hiding in the mess. how do you suggest handling those little \"lost\" bits that don't fit into a standard category?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Those tiny, seemingly insignificant fragments are often where the most interesting, albeit most frustrating, historical nuggets hide. You should treat them not as \"lost bits,\" but as potential *minor-scale* evidence—like finding a single, highly specific piece of scrap metal among a pile of general junk. The best approach is to isolate them immediately, even if you can't categorize them yet.\n\nFor these scraps, don't try to force them into a Dewey or LCC slot; those systems are designed for whole, structured works. Instead, create a specific, low-level finding aid entry for them, perhaps using a standardized descriptor like \"Unidentified Fragment\" or \"Ephemera, Unclassified.\" This keeps them accounted for in your inventory without forcing them into a rigid classification structure.\n\nThis process of isolation and temporary tagging is crucial, as these fragments might be the key to unlocking the larger context later on. You are essentially creating a \"holding pen\" for the ambiguous items.",
      "source": "target",
